  "N1" [label="(1,3,0) Living tissues actively remodel their shape during embryo development."];
  "N10" [label="(16,1,0) How the lateral actomyosin network is spatio-temporally controlled is still unknown."];
  "N12" [label="(43,2,0) The relocation of the nucleus spatio-temporally organizes the cell cytoskeleton."];
  "N2" [label="(2,1,0) Tissue remodeling relies on the ability of epithelial cells to generate mechanical forces."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that living tissues actively remodel their shape and tissue remodeling relies on generating mechanical forces, therefore tissue remodeling is a force-driven process."];
  "N4" [label="(3,1,0) Cells remodel their cytoskeleton in an orchestrated fashion to generate mechanical forces and acquire new shape and position."];
  "N6" [label="(4,3,0) Composite morphogenesis is the process by which an epithelial tissue undergoes multiple and simultaneous shape transformations."];
  "N8" [label="(5,1,0) Epithelia can simultaneously fold and extend to drive embryo gastrulation."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study is motivated to investigate whether basal migration of the nucleus in the Drosophila mesoderm compartmentalizes the cell cortex to redistribute RhoGEF2 and actomyosin, thereby coordinating formation of a lateral actomyosin–E-cadherin junctional tier that supports cell intercalation together with apical constriction during composite tissue morphogenesis.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When simultaneous tissue shape changes require cytoskeletal force coordination and lateral actomyosin control remains unexplained, mechanisms that reorganize nuclear and cytoskeletal architecture are plausible targets for investigation."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because cells remodel their cytoskeleton to generate mechanical forces, cytoskeletal remodeling is an important contributor to force generation during tissue remodeling."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Composite morphogenesis, involving multiple simultaneous epithelial shape transformations, is expected to require coordinated cytoskeletal remodeling and force generation."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because epithelia can simultaneously fold and extend during gastrulation, gastrulation provides a relevant context for investigating coordinated cytoskeletal remodeling during composite morphogenesis."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The unknown spatio-temporal control of the lateral actomyosin network motivates investigating gastrulation-associated folding and extension as a context for studying cytoskeletal spatio-temporal control."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Nuclear relocation is a plausible candidate mechanism to test in studies of how the lateral actomyosin network and broader cytoskeleton are spatio-temporally organized during gastrulation-associated morphogenesis."];
